{"name":"Fading Ember","hex":"#df691e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fading","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of fade."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process by which something fades; gradual diminishment.","An Irish dance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fading"},{"term":"ember","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of coal or wood glowing by heat; a hot coal.","Smoldering ash."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Making a circuit of the year or the seasons; recurring in each quarter of the year, as certain religious days set apart for fasting and prayer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ember"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fading","blocks":[{"text":"In wireless communications, fading is the variation of signal attenuation over variables like time, geographical position, and radio frequency. Fading is often modeled as a random process. In wireless systems, fading may either be due to multipath propagation, referred to as multipath-induced fading, weather, or shadowing from obstacles affecting the wave propagation, sometimes referred to as shadow fading."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/330px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fading"},{"title":"Ember","blocks":[{"text":"An ember, also called a hot coal, is a hot lump of smouldering solid fuel, typically glowing, composed of greatly heated wood, coal, or other carbon-based material. Embers can exist within, remain after, or sometimes precede, a fire. Embers are, in some cases, as hot as the fire which created them. They radiate a substantial amount of heat long after the fire has been extinguished, and if not taken care of properly can rekindle a fire that is thought to be completely extinguished and can pose a fire hazard. In order to avoid the danger of accidentally spreading a fire, many campers pour water on the embers or cover them in dirt. Alternatively, embers can be used to relight a fire after it has gone out without the need to rebuild the fire – in a conventional fireplace, a fire can easily be relit up to 12 hours after it goes out, provided that there is enough space for air to circulate between the embers and the introduced fuel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Embers_01.JPG/330px-Embers_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ember"},{"title":"Photodegradation","blocks":[{"text":"Photodegradation is the alteration of materials by light. Commonly, the term is used loosely to refer to the combined action of sunlight and air, which cause oxidation and hydrolysis. Often photodegradation is intentionally avoided, since it destroys paintings and other artifacts. It is, however, partly responsible for remineralization of biomass and is used intentionally in some disinfection technologies. Photodegradation does not apply to how materials may be aged or degraded via infrared light or heat, but does include degradation in all of the ultraviolet light wavebands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Fotodegradazione_secchio_plastica_usato_come_vaso_da_fiori.png/330px-Fotodegradazione_secchio_plastica_usato_come_vaso_da_fiori.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photodegradation"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_time.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/1280px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_bandwidth.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bonfire in rural Australia, with a large number of sparks being blown by the wind; sparks are akin to embers in a similar way that dust is to stone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonfire_Embers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/1280px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Embers of firewood used in sauna stove","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi